1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:00,942
Up all night?

2
00:00:00,942 --> 00:00:04,453
Here's what really happens to your body after one sleepless night.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:10,500
Sleep is when your brain stores memories, and scientists think it also clears out waste.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:12,589
Skip it, and focus and memory slip.

5
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:19,775
After about 17 hours awake, studies show your reactions can be about as slow as after drinking alcohol.

6
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:25,509
Your brain may slip into microsleeps: a few seconds of sleep you don't even notice.

7
00:00:25,509 --> 00:00:28,053
That's why drowsy driving is so dangerous.

8
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:33,614
Your hunger hormones shift too, so you tend to crave sugary, fatty snacks the next day.

9
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:36,672
Emotions get harder to control.

10
00:00:36,672 --> 00:00:40,083
You may feel more irritable, anxious or low than usual.

11
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:44,642
Good news: for most people, one bad night isn't harmful long term.

12
00:00:44,642 --> 00:00:47,159
A good night's sleep helps you bounce back.

13
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:49,876
Adults need about 7 to 9 hours.

14
00:00:49,876 --> 00:00:54,495
If you often can't sleep or feel exhausted for weeks, talk to a doctor.
